kernel: module parameters from xnu boot-args; module! without ELF sections

A kext has no `__param` section walker, no initcall sections, no modprobe and no sysfs, and
LinuxKPI's struct kernel_param/kernel_param_ops have another layout (no flags, no arg union).
The two section names are also not valid Mach-O section specifiers, so `module!` does not
even compile for this target as it is.

With cfg(sf_lkpi):
  - module_param: `ModuleParamAccess<T>` and `ModuleParam` are unchanged (same `.value()`),
    the kernel_param plumbing is compiled out, and `sf_set_from_boot_arg()` reads the
    boot-arg `<module>.<param>=value` through the same parser Linux uses.
  - module!: no `__param` and initcall statics; `__<name>_init()` reads every parameter's
    boot-arg before the module's own init. `__<name>_init()`/`__<name>_exit()` stay
    `#[no_mangle] extern "C"`: the kext's start/stop routines call them.
Without cfg(sf_lkpi) nothing changes.

